WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.720
If you look at a map, Perth sits on a

2
00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:04.320
lonely edge of the world.

3
00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:07.120
It's walled off by the Indian Ocean to the
west

4
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.320
and thousands of miles of completely empty
desert to the

5
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.200
east. In the early 1990s, that extreme
geographic isolation created

6
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.200
a massive physical barrier.

7
00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:19.520
If you lived there and wanted to hear the
emerging

8
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.760
global electronic music vanguard, sounds
like Detroit Techno or European

9
00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.600
Acid, you couldn't rely on touring DJs or
well-stocked local

10
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.800
record bins. Every piece of that music had
to cross

11
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:32.160
the world physically.

12
00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.920
Vinyl records were pressed overseas,
loaded onto freight ships, and

13
00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:39.440
distributed through a tiny handful of
specialist local shops.

14
00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:43.440
Meanwhile, commercial radio stations
actively excluded the genre.

15
00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.000
Synthetic, repetitive electronic beats had
absolutely no place in mainstream

16
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.240
daytime broadcasts. That combination of
distance and a total mainstream

17
00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.360
blackout created a severe cultural vacuum.

18
00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:58.880
A local community, starved for these
sounds, was forced to

19
00:00:58.880 --> 00:00:59.600
build its own.

20
00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:04.520
localized transmission lifeline That
lifeline came from the FM dial,

21
00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:10.120
92.1, RTR-FM. As a community station,
their explicit mission was

22
00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:14.120
to act as the sound alternative,
platforming the marginalized audio

23
00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.040
that commercial stations ignored.

24
00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:19.080
But keeping that music on the air required
immense manual

25
00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:23.480
effort. Unpaid volunteer presenters
carried their own heavy record crates,

26
00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:26.200
mixers, and turntables across the city in
the dead of

27
00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:30.120
night, building a temporary club setup
inside the radio booth.

28
00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:33.800
Early pioneers fought hard to push
electronic music into daytime

29
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:35.240
and drivetime slots.

30
00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:38.680
Winning those mainstream battles provided
the institutional cover needed for

31
00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:41.800
more experimental sounds to take root in
the shadows, which

32
00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:43.960
paved the way for auditory spiral.

33
00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:47.880
Architected by Dejays Seamus and Nikolai,
the program colonized the

34
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:49.320
Sunday midnight to 6 a.m.

35
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:52.440
slot with a signature aesthetic that
listeners described as deep

36
00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:57.360
and dark. Commercial broadcasters call the
overnight slot dead air.

37
00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.720
Here, it was a zone of absolute creative
freedom.

38
00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:04.560
Stripped of demographic pressures, these
DJs could let a 12-minute

39
00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:10.880
minimal track slowly unfold, constructing
hypnotic, unbroken six-hour sonic journeys.

40
00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:14.480
Far from the centralized transmission
tower, out in the sprawling,

41
00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:18.160
darkened suburbs, an audience was waiting
by their stereos.

42
00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:21.040
Listeners would sit poised with blank
cassette tapes.

43
00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:24.160
The precise moment a compelling beat
emerged from the broadcast

44
00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.720
static, they would manually hit record.

45
00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.320
These home-dubbed recordings became prized
physical artifacts.

46
00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:34.400
Each one was carefully labeled,
duplicated, and traded by hand.

47
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:37.680
Because it was nearly impossible to buy
obscure European imports

48
00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.840
locally, manual tape trading functioned as
an analog peer-to-peer distribution

49
00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:46.800
network. This growing network bypassed
traditional retail, circulating directly from

50
00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.000
person to person through high schools and
subversive clothing outlets.

51
00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:55.560
Those ephemeral overnight radio waves
achieved localized permanence through analog

52
00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:00.520
archiving. A single broadcast bridged the
isolating geography between hundreds

53
00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:03.160
of listeners, connecting them physically.

54
00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:06.360
But this community couldn't easily take
their tapes to commercial

55
00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:11.000
nightclubs. The aggressive tempos and
synthetic textures of pure techno

56
00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:15.720
were routinely rejected, forcing listeners
to materialize their own autonomous

57
00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:20.680
physical zones. The music manifested in
surreal, improvised venues.

58
00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:24.920
Raves were held deep inside abandoned
industrial spaces, or in

59
00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.840
one famous instance, a Star Wars-themed
party thrown seven floors

60
00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:31.560
underground in a concrete Wilson car park.

61
00:03:31.560 --> 00:03:35.080
The subculture built a distinct identity
around a philosophy of

62
00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:37.800
peace, love, unity, and respect.

63
00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:42.760
Their uniform prioritized endurance over
aesthetics, excessively baggy clothing, and

64
00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:46.400
obscure brand markers, like the dancing
techno figure stitched onto

65
00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.320
a pair of non-issue jeans.

66
00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:52.240
Auditory Spiral acted as the vital
marketing channel and central

67
00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:57.040
nervous system for this clandestine
movement, providing its essential post-party

68
00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:02.240
soundtrack. The cassette network
functioned as an open-source audio syllabus,

69
00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:05.520
providing the Sunday night community with
a technical blueprint of

70
00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:07.520
global electronic music.

71
00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:11.840
By obsessively analyzing the structures,
tempos, and textures of these

72
00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:16.880
unbroken overnight broadcasts, a whole
generation of future DJs, promoters,

73
00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:19.280
and producers learned their craft.

74
00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:24.240
This self-taught underground education
shows that true innovation doesn't require

75
00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:26.400
proximity to a global epicenter.

76
00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:29.920
It relies on the dedication of curators
willing to broadcast

77
00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:34.000
difficult art. Perth remains one of the
most physically isolated

78
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.840
cities on Earth, yet, through a simple FM
frequency, its

79
00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:42.200
residents tapped directly into a
trans-hemispheric vanguard of sound.

80
00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:46.520
The most enduring cultural movements are
forged out of absolute

81
00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:51.160
necessity, requiring champions who
literally carry the physical weight of

82
00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:55.320
the medium across the city to keep the
signal alive.

83
00:04:55.320 --> 00:05:00.280
The legacy of Perth's electronic music
underground proves that culture-defining

84
00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:03.400
moments rarely happen in the daytime
mainstream.

85
00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:06.200
They are built at the extreme margins of
the dial,

86
00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.680
in the quietest hours of the night.

